WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.367
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:09.920
What was your first
computer science textbook?

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.690
I feel like it was called,
like, Java by Construction,

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.610
was my first textbook.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.610
It was like an introduction
to programming in Java,

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.600
which I took in a freshman
coding class at Princeton.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.530
My mom gave me a textbook when
I was about eight years old,

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.610
an introduction to Python.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.970
And she started reading it
to me when I was in class,

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.040
and that was a
huge moment for me.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.670
I got a textbook on MATLAB
programming, I think.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.060
And I don't know if I ever
cracked the spine of it once.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.780
CLRS textbook, the
algorithms textbook

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.170
that they use in the
algorithms class here at MIT--

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:52.610
it's definitely like a
mammoth of a textbook,

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.880
but I feel like it's sort
of a rite of passage.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:57.980
I wasn't a huge
fan of textbooks.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.410
I mostly read the
course notes that were

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:01.770
posted in a lot of classes.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:05.180
But there's an introduction
to algorithms textbook I used.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:11.570
I think it's by one of the
Kleinbergs, and Eva Tardos

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:12.810
as well.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.595
And that was a great textbook.

00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:16.470
I love that textbook.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.240
And it's really fun,
because each problem

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.470
is sort of structured
as like a little puzzle

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.590
that you have to solve.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.580
So that was my first
textbook that I enjoyed.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.480
You know, I don't think I've
ever used a computer science

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:30.600
textbook.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.620
When I really think through all
of my computer science classes,

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.320
maybe they assigned a
textbook, but I didn't get it.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.685
Most of the time the problem
sets were available online.

00:01:38.685 --> 00:01:40.310
The lectures were
all available online.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.102
And most of what you
learn is from Googling

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:43.893
all the different
questions you might have,

00:01:43.893 --> 00:01:45.380
and other people
telling you from--

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.610
maybe, I guess,
textbook-wise, Wikipedia.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.735
Wikipedia is my favorite
computer science textbook.

00:01:49.735 --> 00:01:52.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

